# Docker ignore rules are not gitignore rules. A bare entry here is anchored at
# the build-context root and `*` never crosses `/`, so `__pycache__` only ever
# matched the single directory at the root -- every nested one rode into the
# image. Measured 2026-08-20 on a real owner context: 175 `__pycache__` paths
# (23MB) plus the whole 224MB `artifacts/` tree, and each of those lands twice,
# once in `COPY . .` and once in the `chown -R /app` layer above it.
# Anything that has to match at any depth is written with a leading `**/`.
# `docker/agent-gateway.Dockerfile.dockerignore` already got this right; this
# file did not.

# Version control and sibling worktrees
.git
.worktrees

# Python environments, bytecode and tool caches
**/.venv
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc
**/.pytest_cache
**/.mypy_cache
**/.ruff_cache

# Node dependency trees. The web bundle is built in its own stage, which
# installs from the lockfile instead of copying an installed tree.
**/node_modules

# Test and browser-tool output
**/test-results
**/playwright-report
.playwright-cli

# Host-side only, and already the case before this change. The OSS provenance
# guards read `docs/oss/*` from the worktree, never from the image, and
# `external/` is vendored reference source. `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md` is not
# listed here and stays in the image. Both entries are deliberately left
# root-anchored: a package that ships its own `docs/` directory should keep it.
docs
external

# Regenerable working output. `CLAUDE.md` §5 and fast-path §10.16 already treat
# this tree as rebuildable, and nothing inside the container reads it -- the
# owner-ready receipt under `artifacts/owner-ready` is written by the host
# PowerShell script (`scripts/owner-ready.ps1`), never by the image. Root-
# anchored on purpose: `RESEARCH/*/artifacts/` is a checked-in claim ledger.
artifacts

# Large local media and databases, at any depth. No tracked file in this
# repository uses these extensions; they only ever appear as working output.
**/*.sqlite
**/*.mp4
**/*.wav
**/*.mp3
